@node Uptime
@chapter Uptime
@cindex uptime
@cindex boot history
@cindex power cut

The @dfn{Uptime} screen shows how long each machine of the
@code{[machines]} table has been running and when the electricity last
went out.  It has two parts.

The upper frame is a table with one row per configured host: a status
dot (green when the host answers, red when it does not, grey while it
is first being asked or when it is not polled at all), its name, its
kind, its address, its uptime (@samp{up 14h 8m}, or @samp{down 3m} with
the time since it last answered), its last boot in local time
(@samp{booted Mon 05 Oct 06:38}) and the number of boots in the period
the timeline covers.  The timeline at the right covers the last seven
days, one column per slice of time, with the day names on the header
line: @samp{━} while the host was up, @samp{┃} in yellow at a boot,
@samp{┃} in red at a boot that belongs to a power cut, @samp{╌} after a
host that no longer answers was last heard from, and dots where nothing
is known.  On a narrow terminal the kind and then the address columns
make room for the timeline.

The lower frame, @samp{Power}, starts with the last power cut:

@example
Last power cut: Mon 05 Oct 06:36 (router, hppavillion restarted), 14h ago
@end example

@noindent
and, when some answering host booted before it, which hosts kept
running through it (a Pi on a UPS, for example).  Below are two lists:
the power cuts found, newest first, and every recorded boot, with the
ones that belong to a power cut marked.

The screen does not poll the hosts itself: it reads the snapshots of
the Machines screen's sources (@pxref{Machines}).  Hosts are polled
once even when both screens are shown, in whichever order
@code{[app] screens} lists them; with only @samp{uptime} listed, the
hosts are still polled with the @code{[machines]} settings.

