@node Detecting power cuts
@section How power cuts are detected
@cindex power cut, detection

Every host reports its uptime.  The time of the poll minus the uptime
is the time the host booted.  raspdash records every new boot in a
@dfn{boot history} that is kept in the cache directory, so it survives
restarts and grows over weeks; the last 50 boots of each host are kept.
Two boot times of the same host less than three minutes apart are the
same boot, which absorbs the few seconds a poll takes.  When raspdash
starts without a history, the first answer of each host already gives
its current boot, so the screen shows the last power cut at once if
the hosts were restarted by it.

A power cut restarts several machines at nearly the same time, while a
reboot by hand or after an update restarts one.  The boots of all hosts
are therefore sorted by time and grouped: a group starts at the
earliest boot not yet grouped and takes every boot of another host
within the next ten minutes.  A group is a @dfn{likely power cut}, dated
at its earliest boot, when

@itemize
@item
it contains a boot of the router (a host of kind @code{keenetic}),
since a router practically only restarts when it loses power; or
@item
it contains boots of at least two hosts, and at least one of them has
no battery.
@end itemize

A machine with a battery (one whose statistics include a battery
charge) rides out a power cut, so its boots count only together with
others: two laptops that restart together, for an update for example,
are not a power cut, but a laptop that restarts with the Pi is.  A
single machine without a battery that restarts alone is not counted
either, because it was more likely restarted on purpose.

@cindex clock, Pi without RTC
A Raspberry Pi has no battery-backed clock.  After a power cut it starts
with the time it saved at shutdown and corrects it once NTP answers,
which may be minutes later.  Boot times computed before the correction
are wrong by the size of the jump, so raspdash does not trust the wall
clock alone: it remembers each host's last uptime together with the
time of the Pi's monotonic clock.  When the uptime has grown by as much
as the monotonic time (give or take two minutes), the host has not
restarted, and a boot time that moved is corrected in place instead of
being recorded as a new boot.  A boot recorded with the wrong clock in
the minutes before the correction is therefore fixed rather than
duplicated.  The first answers after raspdash starts have nothing to
compare with and are matched by time only.

@node Uptime configuration
@section Configuration

All keys are optional.

@example
[uptime]
interval = 60
days = 7
keep = 50
same_boot_minutes = 3
window_minutes = 10
# timezone = "Europe/Moscow"
@end example

@table @code
@item interval
Seconds between updates of the saved boot history.  Default 60.  The
screen itself always uses the newest host snapshots.
@item days
How many days the timeline covers.  Default 7.
@item keep
Boots kept per host.  Default 50.
@item same_boot_minutes
Boot times of one host closer than this are the same boot.  Default 3.
@item window_minutes
How close the boots of different hosts must be to count as one power
cut.  Default 10.
@item timezone
The time zone for the boot times and the day names of the timeline.
Default: the system's time zone.
@end table

@node Uptime internals
@section Internals

@file{src/uptime.cpp} holds the source and the screen; the history and
the clustering are pure functions declared in @file{src/uptime.hpp} and
tested by @file{tests/test_uptime.cpp}.

@code{machines::hosts} gives the configured hosts (@pxref{Machines
internals}).  Each @code{machines::Reading} carries the host's
@code{Stats} and the wall-clock and monotonic times of the poll.

@code{BootSource} (named @samp{uptime}) holds a @code{History}, a map
from host name to @code{HostHistory}: the host's kind, whether it has
a battery, its boots in ascending order, and its last
@code{Observation} (wall time, monotonic time and uptime; kept in
memory only).  Each @code{fetch} applies the latest reading of every
host with @code{observe}, publishes the result, and writes
@file{uptime-history} to the cache directory (JSON, boot times as Unix
seconds) when a boot, kind or battery flag changed.  It never fails.
@code{restore} loads the file; @code{fetch} loads it too when it runs
first, as with @option{--check}.

@code{observe} decides whether an observation is the same boot as the
last one (uptime grown by the elapsed monotonic time, with a two-minute
slack) and then either corrects the latest boot when it moved by more
than @code{same_boot}, or adds the boot unless a recorded one is within
@code{same_boot}.  The boots are then sorted, neighbours within
@code{same_boot} merged, and the oldest dropped beyond @code{keep}.

@code{power_events} groups the boots of all hosts as described above
and returns the power cuts newest first, each with its boots in time
order; @code{event_hosts} names them.  @code{timeline} turns a host's
boots, the boots that belong to a power cut, the time it was last seen
and whether it answers now into one @code{Cell} per column.

The screen applies the current readings to a copy of the history on
every redraw, so a boot shows up as soon as a host answers, without
waiting for the source's interval.
